    <span class="accordion__number">1</span>
    Contradiction as the Engine of Development
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
      <h3>Why does anything change?</h3>
<p>Here is a question that sounds simple but isn’t: why does anything change? Not just events — why do ideas change? Why does a society that once accepted slavery eventually find it intolerable? Why does a political system that works well in one era become inadequate in the next? Why does a scientific theory that explained the evidence eventually give way to a better one?</p>
<p>One answer: external pressure. Things change because they are pushed from outside. But Hegel thought this answer was superficial. The more important driver of change, he argued, is internal contradiction. Every idea, institution, or historical moment contains tensions within itself — commitments that point in different directions, principles that are in conflict with their own implications. These internal tensions are what drive development. Change is not primarily imposed from outside; it erupts from within.</p>
<p>This is the core of Hegel’s dialectic. The word “dialectic” comes from the Greek <em>dialektikē</em> — the art of dialogue and argument. But Hegel gives it a much larger meaning: dialectic is the pattern by which anything develops, whether it is an argument, a historical period, a culture, or reality itself.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">2</span>
    Thesis, Antithesis, Synthesis
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
      <h3>The three-beat structure</h3>
<p>The famous shorthand for Hegel’s dialectic is <strong>thesis → antithesis → synthesis</strong>. Hegel himself rarely used these exact terms — he preferred his own vocabulary — but the structure they describe is genuinely his.</p>
<p>The idea works like this. You begin with some position, claim, or historical form — the <strong>thesis</strong>. This position is not simply wrong, but it is incomplete; it contains a one-sidedness, an internal tension, that it cannot resolve on its own terms. That tension generates its opposite — the <strong>antithesis</strong>, which pushes back against the thesis, exposing its limitations. But the antithesis is also one-sided; it has simply flipped the original position rather than transcending it. What is needed is a <strong>synthesis</strong>: a new position that preserves what was true in both the thesis and antithesis while overcoming the limitation that set them against each other.</p>
<p>Crucially, the synthesis is not a compromise — not a splitting of the difference. It is a genuine advance: something that could not have been reached without going through the conflict. Hegel uses the German word <em>Aufhebung</em> — often translated as “sublation” — to describe what happens in the synthesis. <em>Aufheben</em> in ordinary German means simultaneously to cancel, to preserve, and to elevate. The synthesis cancels the conflict, preserves what was valuable in both sides, and elevates understanding to a higher level.</p>

<div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">ℹ️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>An example: freedom and authority</p>
<p><strong>Thesis:</strong> Pure individual freedom — the idea that each person should be entirely self-determining, unbound by any authority or obligation to others.</p>
<p><strong>Antithesis:</strong> Pure authority — the idea that order and community require individuals to subordinate themselves entirely to law, tradition, or the state.</p>
<p><strong>Synthesis:</strong> A rational state in which laws and institutions are not external constraints on freedom but expressions of it — in which being a citizen and being free are not opposites but the same thing. (This is Hegel’s own view of the modern state in the <em>Philosophy of Right</em>.)</p>
</div></div>
<p>And then the synthesis becomes a new thesis — it will have its own tensions, generate its own antithesis, and the process continues. The dialectic is not a single episode but an ongoing movement. For Hegel, this movement is the very structure of reality developing toward its own completion.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
