      <h3>Hegel and Socrates: a shared name, a different move</h3>
<p>You have already encountered the word “dialectic” in Unit 1. The Socratic elenchus — the method of cross-examination that Socrates uses in the dialogues — is sometimes called dialectical: a back-and-forth between positions, driven by questioning, aimed at reaching truth. Hegel’s dialectic is related but importantly different.</p>

<div>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>Socratic Dialectic</th>
<th>Hegelian Dialectic</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Setting</td>
<td>A conversation between two people</td>
<td>The development of ideas, history, or reality itself</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Driver</td>
<td>Questioning that exposes contradiction in a held belief</td>
<td>Contradiction internal to a position that forces development</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Goal</td>
<td>A better definition, or acknowledgment of ignorance</td>
<td>A synthesis that transcends the original opposition</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Scale</td>
<td>An individual argument or inquiry</td>
<td>The movement of thought, culture, or world history</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Both forms of dialectic share the insight that contradiction is productive — that it is not a dead end but a starting point. Socrates uses contradiction to show his interlocutors that they don’t know what they think they know. Hegel uses it to explain how anything new comes into being at all. In both cases, the move through contradiction is not a failure but a necessary step.</p>

